Chapter 4

Operational Protocols: The Revised Facilitation Committee Handbook

The institutional technologies specified in Chapter 3 must be translated into standardised, executable procedures. This chapter presents the revised Facilitation Committee Handbook, the procedural guide that the elected, rotating Committee uses to execute the Constitution’s adaptive processes. The Handbook incorporates the Anti-Capture Mechanisms and Psychological Governance Mandates as binding operational requirements, integrates the CRI into decision flows, and updates the Monthly Review, Restorative Justice, and Annual Convention protocols to reflect the Dual Legitimacy Model and external review requirements.

4.1 Authority, Scope, and Principles

The Facilitation Committee is the settlement’s process steward, not its government. Its authority extends only to the facilitation of constitutional processes; it cannot issue directives, allocate resources, or adjudicate disputes. It ensures that the settlement’s conversations are fair, productive, and properly recorded. The Handbook is the Committee’s primary procedural document; deviation is permitted only in circumstances not contemplated by the Handbook and must be documented with a public justification logged within 24 hours.

The Committee’s operating principles remain: neutrality, transparency, inclusion, and efficiency. To these is added a constitutional obligation to enforce anti-capture rules and psychological governance mandates as non-discretionary duties.

4.2 Mandatory Anti-Capture Provisions

The Committee is itself subject to the Anti-Capture Mechanisms (Section 3.6). The following are operationalised as automatic, non-discretionary procedures:

· Term Limits Enforcement. The outgoing Committee, at the Annual Convention, certifies that all incoming Committee members meet eligibility: no member has exceeded two consecutive terms or four lifetime terms; no incoming member has held Domain Authority during the preceding term; no member served on the Emergency Council in the prior two years. The certification is public.
· Lottery-Based Selection Protocol. When the eligible candidate pool exceeds three, the outgoing Committee conducts a public, verifiably random lottery. The algorithm is published in advance; the random seed is generated from a physical entropy source (e.g., atmospheric noise) witnessed by at least three settlers. This eliminates any suspicion of manipulation.
· Conflict-of-Interest Registry Maintenance. The Committee maintains the public COI registry. Any settler may file a concern via a standard form. The Committee reviews all unreviewed filings at its quarterly meeting. Any member who is the subject of a filing recuses themselves from the review. An undisclosed COI that is later substantiated triggers automatic removal of the individual from all constitutional roles, effective immediately. The removal is logged and reported at the next Monthly Review.
· Competence Distribution Monitoring. The Committee publishes a quarterly “Bus Factor Report” showing, for each safety-critical Domain, the number of certified Domain Holders, the length of time since the last new certification, and any Domain with a sole holder approaching the six-month limit. A Domain with a bus factor below three triggers a mandatory training prioritisation recommendation to the settlement.

4.3 Psychological Governance Mandates

The Committee is responsible for monitoring compliance with the Psychological Governance provisions and for initiating interventions when metrics indicate systemic psychological strain.

· Decompression Cycle Protection. The Committee maintains a public Decompression Calendar showing each settler’s protected sol. Contacting a settler during their protected sol is a violation reportable via a standard incident form. Three substantiated violations against the same settler in a single Earth year trigger a facilitated intervention with the violator, focused on systemic causes (e.g., workload distribution, cultural norms of availability).
· Burnout Monitoring and Intervention. The Committee receives weekly individual burnout indicator data (aggregated and anonymised in the P-index publication). If an individual settler’s indicator crosses the threshold, the Committee must, within 24 hours, initiate the mandatory reduced-duty protocol: the settler is removed from the duty roster for non-life-support tasks, their Domain Authority certifications are temporarily suspended (with coverage from other certified settlers), and they are offered confidential counseling. The settlement’s work schedule is adjusted publicly to normalise the intervention as a systems response, not a personal failing.
· Grief Protocol Activation. Upon notification of a settler death, the Committee immediately suspends all non-life-support duties for three sols, posts a public notice of the protocol activation, and initiates the facilitated grief circle within 24 hours. The deceased’s private quarter is physically sealed by a Committee member witnessed by another settler; the seal may not be broken for 30 sols except under a validly invoked Article I emergency.
· Emotional Overload Leave Processing. A settler self-declaring emotional overload notifies the Committee via text. The notification is accepted without question. The Committee notifies the duty scheduler and the settler’s Domain peers that an unscheduled leave has begun, without stating the reason beyond “constitutional leave activated.” The leave is logged for frequency monitoring; a settler who invokes the right more than six times in a rolling Earth year is offered—not mandated to attend—a supportive conversation with a counselor and a Committee member to explore systemic stressors.
· Silence Right Protection. In all facilitated meetings, the Committee’s opening script must include the statement: “You have the right to remain silent. You may submit written comments at any time. Silence will not be interpreted.” The Committee records “declined to speak” for any round in which a settler passes, and this recording carries no evaluative weight.

4.4 Protocol A: Monthly Constitutional Review (Revised)

The Monthly Review protocol is updated to incorporate CRI data, Domain Authority consultation, and the Dual Legitimacy deliberation structure.

Pre-Meeting (Day 25-30). The solicitation of agenda items now must include the latest CRI reading and trend chart, enabling settlers to submit topics informed by system state. The one-page brief published before the meeting includes: the CRI and its sub-index breakdown, any Domain Authority opinions on the selected topic if it touches a Domain, and framing questions that distinguish between political and technical dimensions.

The Meeting (with CRI-informed decision rules). The facilitator’s script is modified to include a CRI acknowledgment at the opening: “The current Constitutional Risk Index is [value], with trend [improving/stable/declining]. This meeting’s topic is [Topic]. If at any point we reach a proposal, we will consider its CRI implications.” The uninterrupted perspective-sharing round and facilitated discussion proceed as in the original protocol, but with the following additions:

· If the topic falls within a Domain, the Domain Authority holder(s) must be present. Their formal opinion is read aloud before the synthesis phase. The facilitator ensures that any recommendation or amendment that contradicts unanimous Domain Authority advice includes a written justification in the published record.
· During synthesis, the facilitator asks: “Would this proposal, if implemented, be expected to raise, lower, or have no effect on the CRI? Can we quantify the expected effect?” The answer is recorded in the Meeting Record.

Call for Consensus. For a Recommendation, objection triggers Parking Lot listing. For an Amendment Proposal, the facilitator explicitly notes the ratification threshold (2/3 or 4/5) and confirms that the text as drafted is complete.

Post-Meeting. The Meeting Record now includes the CRI at time of meeting, any Domain Authority opinions, and the expected CRI effect of any recommendation.

4.5 Protocol B: Restorative Justice Circle (Revised)

The original Restorative Justice Protocol is hardened with the graduated consequence system, CRI linkage, and the voting weight reduction procedure.

Triggers and Mediator Selection. The random selection of three mediators from the pool of non-involved, trained settlers is conducted by the lottery protocol. The Committee certifies that no selected mediator has a conflict of interest as per the public registry.

The Circle Process. The script and rounds are unchanged, but the facilitator now records whether each involved party participated or refused. Refusal is documented as a fact, not a judgment.

Consequences of Refusal.

1. First Refusal. The Circle proceeds in the individual’s absence. The incident is automatically elevated to the next Monthly Review’s agenda for collective assessment, bypassing the random selection process.
2. Pattern of Refusal (second refusal within one Earth year). The Committee implements the constitutional consequence: the individual’s voting weight in all settlement decisions is reduced by 50% for six months. The implementation is technical: the settlement’s voting software adjusts the individual’s vote multiplier to 0.5. The Committee notifies the community via a public, neutrally worded notice: “Pursuant to the Constitution, [Name]’s voting weight has been adjusted following a second documented refusal to participate in Restorative Justice within a 12-month period. This adjustment will expire on [Date]. The Monthly Review will discuss the systemic circumstances at its next session.”
3. Chronic Refusal and Threat. A third refusal, or any behaviour that the Monthly Review assesses as a survival-level social threat, triggers an Article I review. If the CRI is above 0.6, this requires the 4/5 supermajority with justification. The review may result in a PSM consideration, which follows the Palliative Separation Protocol (Section 4.7).

4.6 Protocol C: Annual Constitutional Convention (Revised)

The Convention protocol integrates anonymous voting under anti-capture rules, Domain Authority briefing, and the publication of the revised Constitution with the year’s CRI history.

Pre-Convention. The docket includes, for each proposal: the originating Monthly Review date, the CRI at time of drafting, any Domain Authority opinions, and a summary of the deliberation. The mandatory briefing session includes a CRI year-in-review presentation and a Domain Authority statement on any amendments touching safety Domains.

Day 2: Voting. The secure, anonymous digital voting system is verified before the Convention by a technical audit committee drawn by lottery from all settlers with relevant competence. The system must:

· Use cryptographic signatures to verify that each vote is cast by an eligible settler.
· Record the vote content in an encrypted log that is decryptable only for audit purposes.
· Display real-time aggregate results (for/against/abstain) without revealing any individual’s vote.
· Be air-gapped from any network with external connectivity.

The voting order is predetermined: amendments to Articles I-III are voted first, as their outcomes may affect the framing of subsequent votes. The required majority is announced before each vote. Results are final upon display; no revote is permitted for the same text in the same Convention.

Day 3: Election. Election of the new Facilitation Committee proceeds under the Anti-Capture provisions. The outgoing Committee publishes the eligibility-filtered candidate list. If more than three qualify, a public lottery is conducted. The new Committee takes office upon publication of the ratified Constitution v[Year+1].

4.7 Palliative Separation Protocol (Operational)

This protocol operationalises the PSM institutional technology (Section 3.3) as the final stage of the graduated consequence system.

Prerequisite Verification. The Facilitation Committee must certify, before any PSM vote, that: all restorative interventions have been documented as exhausted; the individual has been offered and has refused or failed to complete at least two Restorative Justice Circles; and a pattern of behaviour has been established through Monthly Review records that demonstrates persistent, severe threat to collective survival.

Voting and Invocation. The PSM requires a 4/5 supermajority. The vote is conducted using the same anonymous system as the Convention. The specific behaviours constituting the threat are stated in the ballot text; the individual is provided the text 48 hours in advance and may submit a written response that is distributed to all voters.

Independent Review Body Notification. Within 48 hours of a PSM invocation, the Committee transmits the case file to the IRB (Earth-based ethics panel or inter-settlement panel). The transmission includes the full documentation of interventions, the ballot text, the vote tally, and the individual’s written response. The Committee awaits the IRB’s opinion, which must be issued within a time frame specified in the Treaty (Annex G). If the IRB finds the separation unlawful, the settlement must hold a revote within 14 days. The revote is conducted under the same conditions, with the IRB’s opinion distributed to all voters. If the IRB again finds the separation unlawful after a confirming revote, the individual must be released and reintegrated; the settlement may not initiate a new PSM process based on the same behavioural pattern unless new substantiated incidents occur.

PSM Operation. The individual is relocated to the PSM module. The Committee appoints a PSM Liaison (rotated quarterly) responsible for ensuring supply delivery, scheduling medical checks, and processing the delayed text communication. The Liaison may not be a settler who was party to any conflict with the individual. The PSM conditions—6-month review, reintegration protocol—are explained to the individual in writing upon relocation.

Review and Exit. Every six months, the Committee reviews the PSM case. The individual is offered the opportunity to request reintegration. If requested, a settlement vote by simple majority determines the outcome. The Committee must also consider, even without a request, whether the original threat conditions have materially changed. The settlement’s commitment to indefinite care is constitutionally absolute: the PSM is not a death sentence, not a prison, but a quarantine of last resort with dignity.

4.8 Handbook Amendment Log

This Handbook may be amended by a simple majority vote of the Facilitation Committee, provided changes are published for community review seven days prior to the vote and are certified as compatible with the Constitution’s Articles. All amendments are logged here with version, date, and rationale.



Chapter 5

Technical Specification for Social Architecture: From Governance Requirements to Physical and Digital Infrastructure

5.1 Introduction: The Principle of Architectural Determinism

On Mars, every wall is a constitutional decision. The distinction between architecture and law, which on Earth is maintained by the sheer scale and porosity of the built environment, collapses entirely in a sealed habitat where “outside” is lethal and every cubic metre is precious. A corridor that is too narrow forces unwanted physical proximity and generates conflict; a private quarter without adequate acoustic isolation prevents the psychological recovery that Article II (Bodily & Cognitive Autonomy) guarantees; the absence of a neutral mediation space makes the Restorative Justice Protocol physically impossible to execute. Habitat design is therefore not a matter of aesthetic preference or engineering convenience. It is the physical inscription of the constitutional order, and it must be treated with the same rigor as the legal text itself.

This chapter translates the governance requirements developed in Chapters 2 through 4 into mandatory engineering and architectural specifications. It draws on the emerging discipline of space habitat interior design—particularly research on sensory monotony mitigation, psychological well-being in isolated and confined environments, and human-centered space architecture—to specify the physical systems that enable the Adaptive Constitution to function. The chapter addresses six domains: (1) the zoning and layout of the primary habitat, (2) the Palliative Separation Module, (3) sensory and environmental mediation systems, (4) the secure digital governance infrastructure, (5) life-support resilience as a constitutional guarantee, and (6) the phased implementation roadmap. Together, these specifications constitute the physical instantiation of the Dual Legitimacy Model, the Anti-Capture Mechanisms, and the Psychological Governance Mandates that define the constitutional engineering layer.

5.2 Theoretical Foundation: The Psychology of Confined Environments

Before specifying physical dimensions and system requirements, it is necessary to establish the empirical basis for the design choices that follow. The psychological stressors of long-duration isolation and confinement are well-documented in the human factors and behavioral performance literature. NASA’s Human Research Program has systematically identified the five spaceflight hazards—radiation, altered gravity, isolation and confinement, hostile/closed environments, and distance from Earth—and has catalogued their cognitive, performance, psychological, and neurobiological consequences. Of particular relevance to habitat design is the finding that these stressors can be mitigated by “modifying the vehicle environment” through the “introduction of novel stimuli, greenhouses, virtual reality, etc.”. The environment is not a neutral container; it is an active intervention.

Research specifically focused on the interior design of Mars habitats has emerged as a distinct subfield. Jennifer Lane’s ongoing doctoral work at Curtin University, titled Interior MARSitecture, directly investigates “how the interior design of habitats can contribute to the psychological wellbeing of astronauts who will one day inhabit Mars,” drawing on data from “Antarctic stations, the International Space Station and Mars Simulations.” Lane’s research has identified sensory deprivation, monotony, and feelings of confinement as the primary psychological complaints of analog inhabitants, and has proposed that “these problems can be ameliorated by using lighting, colour, scale, material and volumetric qualities of the habitat”. The SAGA Space Architects’ LUNARK project, a full-scale lunar habitat analog tested during a three-month Arctic deployment, was explicitly designed to “counteract monotony, claustrophobia and psychological stress” through a circadian lighting system that programmed daily variation—“some days would be rich and saturated with sunrise and others would be dull and grey”—to prevent sensory numbness and cognitive deterioration. These empirical foundations validate the premise that architecture is a psychological countermeasure, not merely a shelter.

The specification that follows translates these general findings into constitutionally mandated requirements. Each element is traceable to a specific article or protocol of the Adaptive Constitution, so that a failure to meet a specification is not a design flaw but a constitutional violation.

5.3 Primary Habitat Zones and Requirements

The settlement’s internal configuration must provide gradients of space, privacy, and sensory experience that support the full spectrum of human social needs. The layout moves beyond the undifferentiated open-plan designs that characterized early space analog habitats—which participants described as creating a sense of “being watched” and “uncomfortable” exposure—toward a zoned architecture with constitutionally defined purposes.

5.3.1 Private Quarters: The Sanctuary for Autonomy

Article II’s guarantee of bodily and cognitive autonomy requires, in physical terms, a space that is sensorily and acoustically sovereign. The private quarter is the smallest unit of constitutional jurisdiction within the settlement.

