5.6 Digital Governance Infrastructure

The Adaptive Constitution’s democratic processes—Monthly Review voting, Annual Convention amendment ratification, Facilitation Committee elections, Domain Authority certification records, and conflict-of-interest registry maintenance—require a digital infrastructure that is simultaneously secure, transparent, anonymous where required, and air-gapped from external networks.

5.6.1 System Requirements

The settlement’s governance system shall be a Settlement Governance Network (SGN) , a physically air-gapped local network with the following properties:

Requirement Specification Constitutional Basis
Air-gap No physical or wireless connection to any Earth-communication system. All data transfer to Earth passes through a one-way diode (optical) after human review. Article III (sovereignty); Anti-Capture Mechanisms
Cryptographic identity Each settler possesses a unique cryptographic key pair. Public keys are registered in the SGN ledger. Voting is signed but anonymized through a mix-network or zero-knowledge proof protocol. Annual Convention voting; secret ballot guarantee
Immutable ledger All votes, amendment texts, Meeting Records, and COI registry entries are recorded on an append-only, cryptographically chained ledger (a local blockchain or equivalent). Once recorded, entries are immutable. The ledger is publicly readable within the settlement. Transparency mandate; Anti-Capture (no retrospective alteration)
Verifiability Any settler with relevant technical competence may audit the ledger, verify vote tallies, and confirm that their own vote was recorded as cast. Audit tools are available on all SGN terminals. Article III; trust in democratic process
Redundancy The SGN operates on a minimum of three physically separated nodes. Loss of any two nodes does not result in data loss or service interruption. System resilience
Disaster recovery A complete, encrypted backup of the ledger is stored in a radiation-hardened, physically secured vault updated weekly. Recovery procedure requires the Facilitation Committee and two randomly selected settler witnesses. Constitutional continuity

5.6.2 Voting Protocol

The voting protocol specified in Chapter 4 for the Annual Convention is implemented as follows:

1. Pre-vote verification. The SGN publishes the list of eligible voters (all settlers with full voting weight; settlers under voting weight reduction with multiplier indicated). Any settler may challenge the list within 24 hours before voting opens.
2. Vote casting. Each settler authenticates to the SGN using their private key at a dedicated terminal in the Facilitation Chamber. The system presents the proposal text, the Domain Authority opinion (if applicable), and the required majority. The settler selects “For,” “Against,” or “Abstain.” The vote is encrypted, signed, and appended to the ledger.
3. Tallying. At the close of voting, the SGN publishes the tally: total for, against, abstain, and the weighted-adjusted totals accounting for any reduced-weight voters. A cryptographic proof accompanies the tally, enabling independent verification.
4. Audit. Any settler may, within 48 hours of the tally, request an audit of the ledger. The audit process is standardized and requires the presence of the requesting settler, one Facilitation Committee member, and one randomly selected settler witness.

