The revision of the PSM construction threshold from 50 to 30 population reflects a key learning from the Biosphere 2 and Pitcairn Island case studies: destructive personality dynamics can emerge in groups as small as 8–12. Waiting until a population of 50 to construct the PSM leaves an unacceptable gap in the settlement’s social safety infrastructure. The improvised PSM capability in Phase 1 is acknowledged as suboptimal but operationally necessary; its existence, even in improvised form, is constitutionally required.

5.9 Summary

The Technical Specification for Social Architecture is the constitution in three dimensions. Every wall, every sensor, every lighting panel, and every voting terminal is a constitutional commitment. The specifications in this chapter are mandatory, not advisory. A habitat that fails to meet them is not merely suboptimal; it is constitutionally non-compliant, because it cannot support the processes—restorative justice, anonymous voting, decompression, dignified quarantine—that the Adaptive Constitution mandates. The next chapter addresses the external architecture: the legal and diplomatic framework that connects the Martian settlement to Earth and to future Martian settlements, embedding the Dependency Reduction Doctrine, external review, and resource governance in an enforceable treaty structure.

