Chapter 6

The Mars-Earth Treaty: From Asymmetric Sovereignty to Earned Autonomy

6.1 Introduction: The Legal Vacuum

The Adaptive Constitution governs the internal life of the Martian settlement with operational precision. But no settlement is an island—or rather, a settlement is precisely an island, and its survival depends on the legal and material architecture of its relationship with Earth. That relationship currently occupies a near-vacuum in international law. The 1967 Outer Space Treaty (OST), ratified by 118 states and rightly described as “the cornerstone of the international legal regime governing outer space activities,” establishes foundational principles—non-appropriation of celestial bodies by sovereignty claim, freedom of exploration, liability for damage, and state responsibility for national activities—but it is silent on the governance of permanent settlements, the rights of settlers, the status of space-born children, and the mechanisms by which a Martian community might transition from a dependent outpost to a self-governing polity.

This silence is compounded by the fragmentation of contemporary space governance. The Artemis Accords, launched in 2020 and signed by over 60 nations as of April 2026, represent the most ambitious effort to operationalize the OST for the era of commercial lunar and Martian exploration. The Accords explicitly permit resource extraction and establish “safety zones” to prevent harmful interference, but they are non-binding political commitments that function as de facto entry requirements for the U.S.-led Artemis program. They do not address settlement governance. Meanwhile, the UNOOSA Working Group on Legal Aspects of Space Resource Activities, established in 2022, is developing a set of recommended principles through multilateral consensus—a slower but more inclusive process that the European Union has endorsed as “the development of a necessary legal framework”. And the 1979 Moon Agreement, which would have established an international regime for resource exploitation, remains ratified by only a handful of states and is effectively ignored by the major spacefaring powers.

Into this fragmented legal landscape, a permanent Martian settlement must insert itself. It cannot wait for the international community to resolve its disagreements. It must negotiate its existence.

This chapter presents the revised Mars-Earth Preliminary Treaty, a legal instrument designed to govern the settlement’s relationship with Earth during the critical transition from initial dependency to mature autonomy. The Treaty incorporates the Dependency Reduction Doctrine, the Independent Review Body for Palliative Separation, the Resource Partition Framework for inter-settlement relations, and a dispute resolution mechanism grounded in established international arbitration practice. It draws on the most successful model for governing a non-sovereign international space—the Antarctic Treaty System—while acknowledging the profound differences between a continent dedicated to peace and science and a planet where humans intend to live, reproduce, and build a civilization.

6.2 The Antarctic Precedent and Its Limits

The Antarctic Treaty System (ATS), signed in 1959 during the height of the Cold War, has successfully demilitarized an entire continent and set aside territorial claims for nearly seven decades. It is widely considered “one of the most successful international treaties,” a model of how competing powers can cooperate in an extraterritorial space without resorting to sovereignty claims. Its key features—the freezing of territorial claims, the prohibition of military activity, the dedication to scientific research, and consensus-based decision-making among Consultative Parties—have proven remarkably durable.

However, the ATS also faces growing pressures that are directly relevant to the Martian context. Rising geopolitical tensions, particularly between a “Russia-China bloc” and Western nations, have strained consensus-based governance. The expansion of physical presence through station construction acts as a proxy for territorial competition. Tourism pressures and resource exploitation interests (particularly Antarctic krill fishing, which has generated concerns about ecosystem impact and regulatory adequacy) demonstrate that the “continent for peace and science” model frays when economic interests intensify.

The ATS is thus both an inspiration and a warning. It demonstrates that sovereignty claims can be held in abeyance for decades through a framework of mutual restraint, shared scientific purpose, and institutionalized diplomacy. But it also demonstrates that consensus-based governance becomes increasingly fragile as the number of actors and the intensity of their interests grow. A Martian settlement treaty must learn from both the successes and the strains of the Antarctic model.

Crucially, the ATS governs a space where permanent human residence is prohibited (other than scientific personnel on rotation). Mars will host permanent settlers who will raise children, build institutions, and develop a distinct political identity. The ATS model of external governance by distant states is therefore inapplicable in its pure form. The Martian settlement requires something the ATS never contemplated: a structured transition of sovereignty from founding states to the settler community itself.

6.3 The Revised Mars-Earth Preliminary Treaty

Preamble

The Governing Institutions of Earth, hereinafter “Earth,” and the Martian Settlement operating under the Adaptive Constitution, hereinafter “Mars,” recognizing their shared humanity and interdependent destiny, yet acknowledging the profound physical and societal divergence necessitated by their separate environments, and further acknowledging the legal vacuum that currently exists in international space law with respect to permanent human settlements on celestial bodies, hereby establish this Treaty to govern their relationship with mutual respect and asymmetric sovereignty.

The Parties draw inspiration from the Antarctic Treaty System’s successful demilitarization and scientific cooperation while recognizing that a permanent settlement of families, children, and self-governing institutions requires a legal framework that evolves with the settlement’s growing capacity for autonomy. The Parties further take note of the Artemis Accords’ operationalization of the Outer Space Treaty’s resource utilization provisions, the UNOOSA Working Group on Legal Aspects of Space Resource Activities’ ongoing development of multilateral principles, and the International Space Station Intergovernmental Agreement’s successful model of shared governance among sovereign partners.

Article I: Foundational Principles

The four original principles are retained and strengthened:

1. Principle of Mutual Survival. Earth and Mars recognize an irrevocable obligation to preserve the existence of the other as a center of human civilization. This obligation is not merely moral; it is operationalized in the Emergency and Disaster Response Protocol (Annex E).
2. Principle of Asymmetric Sovereignty. Mars is a self-governing, autonomous political entity. Earth possesses no inherent or historical right to impose its laws, customs, or governance structures upon Mars. This principle is philosophically absolute but operationally graduated, as specified in Article VIII (Dependency Reduction Doctrine).
3. Principle of Continuous Negotiation. This Treaty establishes a permanent, structured dialogue through the Treaty Interface System (TIS) to manage the evolving relationship, rejecting static domination in favor of adaptive agreement.
4. Principle of Knowledge as a Commons. Scientific data pertaining to the Martian environment, engineering solutions for extraterrestrial living, and non-personal medical findings shall be shared openly and freely, constituting a protected human legacy. This principle is subject to a limited exception for technologies with dual-use military applications, to be defined in Annex G.
5. Principle of Non-Militarization (New). Consistent with Article IV of the Outer Space Treaty, Mars shall be used exclusively for peaceful purposes, defined as activities that do not involve the deployment of weapons of mass destruction, the establishment of military fortifications, or the conduct of military maneuvers. This provision does not preclude the settlement from maintaining internal security capabilities necessary for the enforcement of its constitutional order.

Article II: Definitions and Scope of Authority

Retained from the original Treaty with the following revisions:

· Martian Jurisdiction: Clarified to include “all persons, vehicles, and structures on the Martian surface, in its atmosphere, and within the Martian Hill Sphere. Mars-born persons are Martian nationals by birth, subject to the Charter of Generational Rights (Adaptive Constitution, Article VI).”
· Terran Jurisdiction: Clarified to include “all launch, transit, and supply activities originating from Earth or Earth orbit, up to the point of handover in Martian space as defined in Annex A.”
· Mixed Jurisdiction: Clarified to include “incidents occurring during transit involving vessels or individuals under dual operation, and all disputes arising from resource extraction activities conducted jointly by Earth-registered entities and Mars-registered entities. Adjudicated per the Dispute Resolution Mechanism (Article VI).”
· Inter-Settlement Relations (New): As additional Martian settlements are established, whether by secession under the Adaptive Constitution’s Article IV or by independent Earth-originating missions, the relationship between settlements shall be governed by a separate Inter-Settlement Compact, with the Resource Partition Framework (Adaptive Constitution, Annex H) as its default resource governance mechanism.

Article III: The Sovereignty Transfer and Governance Interface

The original decaying authority schedule is retained but substantially hardened by integration with the Dependency Reduction Doctrine.

· Initial Authority Gradient (Years 0–5): Earth-based mission control retains advisory authority over technical systems with single-point failure risks. The gradient is:
  · Year 0–2: Earth may issue binding directives for critical life-support systems, with immediate, detailed technical justification linked to CRI-equivalent metrics for the transit and early surface phase.
  · Year 2–5: Earth may issue strong recommendations. Mars may reject them with a documented explanation and a proposed alternative. Rejection of three consecutive Earth recommendations within the same Domain triggers a mandatory mediation under Article VI.
  · Year 5+: Earth provides information and consultation only. Mars holds ultimate decision-making authority over all its operations.
· Governance Interface Protocol: All Earth-originating communications are tagged by the TIS with required response type (Directive, Recommendation, Inquiry) and linked to the relevant section of the decaying authority schedule. Mars-originating communications include the current CRI, enabling Earth to contextualize Martian decisions within system health data.
· Communications Integrity (New): Neither Party may intentionally jam, delay, or censor the communications of the other. The Martian settlement may, however, screen incoming communications for content that poses a demonstrable threat to social stability or psychological health, provided that such screening is transparent, subject to Monthly Review, and does not discriminate based on political viewpoint.

Article IV: Resource Rights and Economic Relations

The original provisions are retained and sharpened:

· Principle of First Use and Stewardship. Mars holds sovereign rights over all local material and energy resources. Extraction and use are governed by Martian law. Mars commits to principles of environmental stewardship informed by the Antarctic Treaty’s Protocol on Environmental Protection, adapted for the Martian context.
· Intellectual Property. Inventions developed independently on Mars are the property of the inventor or their assigned Martian entity under Martian patent law. Earth recognizes Martian intellectual property law, and a cross-filing system is established. Inventions developed jointly or using Earth-originating research infrastructure are subject to a benefit-sharing agreement negotiated under Annex D.
· Trade and Exchange. All exchange is based on mutual agreement, not entitlement. The barter framework (Annex D) is established within the first two years and reviewed biennially.
· Resource Extraction and the Outer Space Treaty (New). The Parties affirm that the extraction and utilization of Martian resources does not constitute “national appropriation” under Article II of the OST, consistent with the interpretation advanced by the Artemis Accords and the domestic legislation of multiple spacefaring nations. However, Mars commits to reporting all resource extraction activities to the UNOOSA registry and to participating in the Working Group on Legal Aspects of Space Resource Activities as an observer, with the aim of contributing to the development of a multilateral governance framework.

Article V: Communication, Movement, and Emergency

Retained with the following additions:

· Communication Protocol. A minimum weekly summary transmission in both directions is mandatory. The transmission includes: Mars-to-Earth (CRI, population update, significant governance events, resource status) and Earth-to-Mars (relevant scientific updates, policy changes affecting the settlement, supply mission schedules). The CRI forms the standardized health reporting mechanism.
· Movement of Persons. Emigration to Mars and immigration to Earth proceed as originally specified, with the addition that a Mars-born person who has attained participatory sovereignty (age 16) may independently petition for immigration to Earth without parental consent, consistent with the Charter of Generational Rights.
· Emergency and Disaster Response. The Joint Emergency Response Protocol (Annex E) is strengthened by the inclusion of pre-authorized decision matrices that enable Earth to dispatch emergency supplies or evacuation capability upon confirmation of CRI < 0.3 or equivalent catastrophic indicators, without waiting for a Martian request that may not be possible to send.

Article VI: Dispute Resolution and Treaty Amendment

Retained but with a revised arbitration mechanism:

· First Stage: Mandatory mediation by a panel of three neutrals: one selected by Earth, one by Mars, and one agreed upon by both. Mediation must conclude within 90 Earth days.
· Second Stage: Binding arbitration before the Permanent Space Arbitration Panel (PSAP), a standing body of five jurists with expertise in international space law, appointed for renewable five-year terms. The PSAP’s composition and rules of procedure are defined in Annex F. Its decisions are binding on both Parties.
· Treaty Amendment: By written agreement of both Parties, ratified according to their respective domestic processes. Amendments affecting Articles I or II, or the Dependency Reduction Doctrine milestones, require a supermajority or referendum in each Party’s governance system.
· Review Clause: Full review every ten Earth years, or upon the occurrence of a major triggering event (Martian population exceeding 1,000; discovery of extraterrestrial life; collapse of a major Earth government signatory; achievement of any Dependency Reduction Milestone).
· Withdrawal (New): Either Party may withdraw from this Treaty upon five Earth years’ written notice. During the notice period, all obligations remain in force, and the Parties shall negotiate a successor agreement. This provision acknowledges the political reality that no treaty can bind a polity in perpetuity, while ensuring that withdrawal is not impulsive and occurs within a structured transition.

Article VII: Final Provisions

Retained.

Article VIII: Dependency Reduction Doctrine (New)

This Article, introduced in Chapter 3 as an institutional technology, is now embedded in the Treaty text as its operational engine. Asymmetric sovereignty is philosophically absolute but physically contingent. The Dependency Reduction Doctrine defines the path from the former to the latter.

· Milestone 1: Food Independence. ≥95% caloric self-sufficiency. Target: Year 7. Metric: (Calories produced on Mars) / (Total calories consumed), verified by independent audit.
· Milestone 2: Manufacturing Independence. ≥80% of replacement parts manufacturable on Mars. Target: Year 10. Metric: Independent assessment of parts producibility from in-situ resources.
· Milestone 3: Medical Independence. All emergency medicine, ≥80% of chronic care, and basic surgical capability available without Earth consultation. Target: Year 12. Metric: Independent medical capability audit.
· Milestone 4: Launch Independence. Mars-origin launch capability to orbit using Mars-manufactured vehicle and Mars-produced propellant. Target: Year 15. Metric: Successful launch and safe return demonstration.

Upon verification of a Milestone by an independent audit panel (one member appointed by Earth, one by Mars, one by mutual agreement), Mars may petition for accelerated decay of Earth’s advisory authority in the relevant domain. Earth may not unreasonably withhold recognition. A dispute over Milestone verification is subject to the Dispute Resolution Mechanism (Article VI).

· Sovereignty Acceleration. Achievement of Milestone 4 (Launch Independence) triggers a mandatory Treaty Review Conference at which Mars may petition for full political sovereignty, defined as the termination of all residual Earth advisory authority and the recognition of Mars as a fully self-governing polity in international law. Earth’s consent shall not be unreasonably withheld.
· Dependency Reporting. Mars shall publish an annual Dependency Index. Earth shall publish an annual assessment. Discrepancies trigger mediation.

