Appendix A: The Constitutional Risk Index — Full Specification

A.1 Definition and Formula

The CRI is a composite metric calculated weekly by the Facilitation Committee and published publicly. It is defined as:

CRI = 0.3(O) + 0.25(W) + 0.2(E) + 0.15(P) + 0.1(C)

where each sub-index is normalized to the range [0, 1], with 1 representing full design specification and 0 representing catastrophic failure.

A.2 Sub-Index Definitions

O (Oxygen System Stability Index)

· Components: O₂ partial pressure reserve margin above minimum habitable threshold (0–1 normalized); redundancy status of primary and backup generators (0.5 each if both operational); maintenance backlog severity weighted by consequence of failure (0–1, with 1 = no backlog).
· Measurement frequency: Continuous sensor data, reviewed weekly.
· CRI impact: O < 0.4 triggers automatic Article I invocation, irrespective of other indices.

W (Water Reserve Security Index)

· Components: Days of full consumption reserve (normalized to 60-day buffer = 1.0); recycling efficiency ratio (actual vs. design specification); contamination risk level from latest water quality panel.
· Measurement frequency: Weekly.
· CRI impact: W < 0.5 triggers facilitated Article I review.

E (Energy Resilience Index)

· Components: Grid redundancy factor (0–1, with dual independent sources = 1.0); battery storage in hours of full demand (72 hours = 1.0); generation source diversity index (Herfindahl-Hirschman inverted).
· Measurement frequency: Weekly.
· CRI impact: E < 0.5 triggers facilitated Article I review.

P (Psychological Health Index)

· Components: Aggregate settlement mood from anonymous weekly survey (Likert scale, normalized); frequency of Restorative Justice Circle triggers (rolling 90-day count, normalized); number of settlers with burnout indicator above threshold; PSM occupancy (0 if vacant, 0.4 if occupied).
· Measurement frequency: Weekly for survey and burnout; continuous for RJ triggers and PSM.
· CRI impact: P < 0.3 triggers mandatory psychological governance review and suspension of all non-life-support overtime.

C (Conflict Escalation Metric)

· Components: Number of active voting weight reductions (rolling count, normalized); frequency of unresolved Parking Lot items older than two months; count of Article I invocations in prior 90 days.
· Measurement frequency: Weekly.
· CRI impact: C < 0.2 triggers mandatory Monthly Review topic selection on conflict escalation.

A.3 Invocation Decision Tree

1. Automatic Invocation (CRI < 0.4): Emergency Council convened without vote. Binding orders may be issued for 72 hours, renewable only with renewed CRI < 0.4.
2. Facilitated Invocation (0.4 ≤ CRI < 0.6): Any settler may petition; simple majority vote required.
3. Restricted Invocation (CRI ≥ 0.6): 4/5 supermajority with written justification required.

A.4 Publication and Audit

The CRI is displayed on all public settlement displays. The raw data for each sub-index is available to any settler. The CRI history is reviewed at the Annual Convention.

